{"author":{"id":"7c5527b9befade3b1be0c1d677ac996815288d8332f34269730a5b81425ee5be"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792395127,"nonce":"dUPlL4Ji5RR/tssuiKlFuZzP6oI=","metadata":{"github-url":"https://github.com/EPFL-ENAC/vps-mitsi/issues/54","origin":"github","sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:id":"I_kwDOULtcMc8AAAABYNhfZg","sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:labels":"","sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:number":"54","sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:status":"open","sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:text":"38c52062c42535628a73603377854a17aff5f3ec1e8cad614e9199c202cbd5f9","sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:title":"[TASK](CI) Automatic dependency updates and security scans","sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:url":"https://github.com/EPFL-ENAC/vps-mitsi/issues/54"},"title":"[TASK](CI) Automatic dependency updates and security scans","message":"**TL;DR**\n- **What:** keep dependencies up to date automatically, and scan for vulnerabilities and leaked secrets.\n- **Why:** nothing watches our dependencies today. The live app ships a `quasar` version with a critical vulnerability and a `vue` version with a high one.\n- **User impact:** nothing visible; fewer known security holes on the live site.\n- **Effort:** small.\n\n---\n\n## What we have today\n- **No dependency updates.** There's no `.github/dependabot.yml`, so packages and GitHub Actions only move when someone bumps them by hand.\n- **Known vulnerabilities go unnoticed.** `npm audit` on `dev` reports 27 (2 critical, 19 high). 6 of them are in what ships to users (`npm audit --omit=dev`): `quasar` (critical), `vue`, `@vue/server-renderer`, `postcss`, `nanoid` and `source-map-js` (high). All 6 have a fix available.\n- **No code or secret scanning.** CodeQL isn't set up, and GitHub secret scanning and push protection are off, although the repo is public, so they're free.\n\n## Why it matters\nThe app is public. Vulnerable packages ship to every visitor, and a key leaked in a public repo is found by bots within minutes.\n\n## What we propose\nGitHub's built-in features first, workflow code only where they don't reach.\n1. **Fix the current findings** (`npm audit fix`, bump `quasar` and `vue`), so the new checks start green.\n2. **Dependabot version updates:** `.github/dependabot.yml` with `npm` for `/frontend` and `github-actions` for `/`, targeting `dev`. Cooldowns as in co2-calculator (7 days default, 30 major, 7 minor, 3 patch), majors ignored. Add the `docker` ecosystem only if the image stays (#40).\n3. **Repo settings** (admin, no code): turn on Dependabot security updates, secret scanning with push protection, and CodeQL default setup for `javascript-typescript`. Dependabot alerts are already on.\n4. **`npm audit` on every PR:** one step in `quality-check.yml` running `npm audit --audit-level=high` (scope: Decision 1).\n\n## Decisions needed\n1. Should the PR check fail on vulnerabilities in dev-only tools (Vite, ESLint…), or only in what ships (`--omit=dev`)? Recommended: **only what ships**. Dependabot alerts still report the rest.\n\n## Done when\n- [ ] The `npm audit` step passes on `dev`.\n- [ ] Dependabot opens update PRs against `dev` for npm and GitHub Actions.\n- [ ] Secret scanning with push protection, Dependabot security updates and CodeQL are on in the repo settings.\n- [ ] A PR that adds a dependency with a known high vulnerability gets a red check.\n\n## Alternatives considered\nA `security.yml` workflow like co2-calculator's (npm audit, CodeQL and TruffleHog, weekly). Push protection blocks a secret before it reaches GitHub, while TruffleHog only finds it after the push, and CodeQL default setup needs no workflow file. A workflow is only worth it if we want the same setup in private repos.\n\n## Technical notes\n- Replaces the security part of #39 (closed by #50, which delivered the quality workflow). The ESLint part is #53. Branch protection is out of scope for now, so a red check warns but doesn't block a merge.\n- Audit numbers measured on `dev` at `709194c` on 2026-10-19.\n- The default branch is `main`, while the team merges into `dev`: check which branch Dependabot security-update PRs open against before turning them on.\n- Docker image scanning (Trivy, run by the shared deploy action) depends on what happens to the Docker path (#40). `deploy.yml` still uses `v3.1.0`; the new `mirror-to-gitlab.yml` already uses `v3.10.0`.\n- Reference: `EPFL-ENAC/co2-calculator` `.github/dependabot.yml`.","files":null}]}